---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import { brand } from "../brand/config";
---

<Base
  title="Correspondence"
  description="How the Society writes to you, how often, and how the letters are produced."
  canonicalPath="/correspondence/"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Correspondence</h1>
      <p>
        The Society's relationship is mostly conducted by mail — physical at the higher tiers,
        digital at every tier. Every letter is personalised within the boundaries you have set.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cadence of the correspondence is real. The cadence of ordinary events is intentional,
        and the cadence of cancellations is part of the cadence. You will receive invitations,
        reminders, cancellations, birthday cards, anniversary recognition, and (at A$20 and A$50)
        signed physical letters.
      </p>

      <h2>What you receive</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Welcome.</strong> A welcome email immediately on joining the waiting list. A welcome
          letter and physical pack on becoming a paying member.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Invitations and cancellations.</strong> Pairs of letters. Invitations arrive roughly
          within the cadence you set. Cancellations arrive before the date. The calendar message uses
          the same UID across invitation and cancellation so your calendar app updates rather than duplicates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Birthday and anniversary recognition.</strong> At every tier on the digital side. At
          A$20 and A$50 on the physical side as well. At A$50 with calls enabled, an opt-in birthday call
          is offered.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Newsletter.</strong> A short, slow, mostly-text newsletter when there is something to
          say. The newsletter is opt-out. Essential cancellation and operational email cannot be opted
          out of.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Operator tasks (your inbox at the {brand.shortName}).</strong> When the Society needs
          to print, sign, post, call, or select a gift, the operator receives the task. Member communication
          is not the same as operator communication.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How letters are produced</h2>
      <p>
        Letter copy is written by the {brand.shortName} using a small language model. Every letter is
        checked against the member's confirmed facts, their do-not-mention list, and the {
          brand.shortName
        }'s editorial voice. The {brand.shortName} never invents facts about you. If the model produces
        a letter that mentions anything you have asked the {brand.shortName} not to mention, the letter
        is rejected and rewritten before it is sent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Physical letters are produced by a human operator. The operator signs, seals, and posts the
        letter. The letter text is checked by the same validator as the digital letter. The
        signature and seal are real. The wax is real. The stamp is real.
      </p>

      <h2>What the Society does not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The {brand.shortName} does not send attendance instructions. No "we can't wait to see you there"
          copy.
        </li>
        <li>
          The {brand.shortName} does not claim fictional characters, locations, or partnerships as real.
        </li>
        <li>
          The {brand.shortName} does not mention the member's previous employer, ex-partner, or anything
          else on the do-not-mention list.
        </li>
        <li>
          The {brand.shortName} does not silently enrich member data from public sources.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Opt-out behaviour</h2>
      <p>
        Opting out of the newsletter does not stop essential cancellation email. Opting out of
        physical mail does not stop digital. Opting out of a single service pauses only that service
        — the rest of the membership is unaffected. Revoking a permission cancels already-scheduled
        future work for that permission. Other actions continue.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
